Geoduck wrote:

 > After a long hiatus, I got interested in NetHack
 > again, and finally updated the Geoduck alternate
 > tileset. It's an attempt to combine the
 > compactness of ACSII with the extra information of
 > tiles. You can get more info at the address in my
 > sig. (Note that many of the links to the GTS still
 > go to my old site, which is a rotting zombie no
 > longer under my control.)

 > The biggest change this time around is that I've
 > gone from 20x10 pixels to 20x12; newer computer
 > monitors have the room, and it helped unclutter
 > the screen a bit, leaving those edge pixels blank.
 > If you're updating to the new version, be sure and
 > change the line in your NH defaults file to the
 > new name/dimensions!

 > NOTE: This set is for "vanilla" NetHack ONLY, NOT
 > SporkHack. If the SporkHack Power(s)-That-Be ever
 > release an official tile-set, I will see what I
 > can do about making a version of mine. I do have a
 > version for Slash'EM available at my site, but it
 > has not been updated this time around.

 > If you've got any suggestions for improvements, or
 > places I should be promoting this, drop me a line!

I just installed and used your new tiles for my
ongoing game, which has enough crud in sight to be a
fair test. Your new tile set looks good, and is very
easy on the eyes. Thanks. I'll be using that version
as my standard tiles from now on.

The only issue I have with the tiles is, the whole
dungeon level looks a little lost on a 1280x800
widescreen laptop.

I gave up on my attempt to make a clean 30x15
version of your tiles, which since it isn't a simple
pixel replication, required redesigning each
individual tile. Trying to do so just makes me
appreciate what an incredible amount of work you put
into your tiles [I got through maybe the lowercase "h"
monsters before I stalled out, so just a bare
beginning] so I've been playing with a fairly cruddy
version of your tiles, just gotten by scaling the
whole bitmap in GIMP to make the individual tiles
30x15, which since it isn't done tile by tile, out
of laziness, spews incredible amounts of color
artifacts across the tile boundaries, and darkens
most of the colors within an individual tile by
blending them with the floor colors, but does leave
me something usable.

That's a better size for the above screen
dimensions, where your 40x20 version didn't quite
fit the whole level on the screen. For other, better
resolution screens, like 1920x1200, your new tiles,
pixel replicated 2x2, would work just fine.

     There's such an astonishing assortment of
